Dear all,

I am trying to calculate the sum of two vectors, one of which is reversed. A very direct way works, such as:
for (k in 1:K) {
  conv[k,1] <- 0
  for(l in 1:(k-1)) {
    conv[k,l+1] <- conv[k,l] + exposure[k-l]*incubationPeriod[l]
  }
  observed[k] ~ dpois(conv[k,k])
}

However, when I try to vectorize it, the fit gives the wrong result. First way is when I write:
for (k in 1:(K-1)) {
  lambda[k+1] <- inprod(exposure[k:1],incubationPeriod[1:k])
  observed[k+1] ~ dpois(lambda[k+1])
}
then the results are not good.

The other way is when I write
for (k in 1:(K-1)) {
  lambda[k+1] <- (exposure[k:1]%*%incubationPeriod[1:k])[1,1]
  observed[k+1] ~ dpois(lambda[k+1])
}
but the kernel dies with the message:
terminate called after throwing an instance of 'std::bad_alloc'
  what():  std::bad_alloc

My question is whether I am correct in my understanding of the inprod-function and making the reversed vector by "k:1". And I am also wondering why I have the message from Cpp in the second variant of vectorization.

Thank you very much for your time in advance.

ps: I may provide a reproducible example if necessary.

