Without addressing how to best do the convoution, I quickly note that "backwards indexing" using a colon (":"), e.g. indexing a vector as x[10:1], does not work in compiled execution of NIMBLE functions.  My (failing) test case is shown below.

Second note, that you *can* do "reverse indexing" of a vector, if the indices themselves are stored in a vector variable.  A (working) example of that is also shown below.

## FAILING EXAMPLE
library(nimble)

Rnf <- nimbleFunction(
    run = function(a = double(1)) {
        L <- dim(a)[1]
        print('length = ', L)
        b <- a[L:1]
        print('reversed vector: ', b)
    }
)

Cnf <- compileNimble(Rnf)

f <- 1:5

Rnf(f)
## length = 5
## reversed vector: 5 4 3 2 1

Cnf(f)
## Process R abort trap: 6 at Fri Aug 31 11:01:27 2018
## length = 5
## libc++abi.dylib: terminating with uncaught exception of type std::bad_alloc: std::bad_alloc

## WORKING EXAMPLE
library(nimble)

Rnf <- nimbleFunction(
    run = function(a = double(1)) {
        L <- dim(a)[1]
        print('length = ', L)
        index <- numeric(L)
        for(i in 1:L) {
            index[i] <- L + 1 - i
        }
        b <- a[index]
        print('reversed vector: ', b)
    }
)

Cnf <- compileNimble(Rnf)

f <- 1:5

Rnf(f)
## length = 5
## reversed vector: 5 4 3 2 1

Cnf(f)
## length = 5
## reversed vector: 5
## 4
## 3
## 2
## 1

